---
import SubjectLayout from "../../layouts/SubjectLayout.astro";
import ScienceSection from "../../components/science/ScienceSection.astro";
import SplitCard from "../../components/science/SplitCard.astro";
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import VideoSlot from "../../components/VideoSlot.astro";
import {
  SLEEP_RHYTHM_SECTION,
  RESTING_HR_SECTION,
  HEART_RATE_RECOVERY_SECTION,
  SLEEP_TRAINING_SECTION,
} from "../../data/science";

/** The four Today tiles, with the rule that colours each one. */
const todayTiles = [
  { label: "Sleep", value: "6h 29", tone: "text-warning", rule: "amber under 7 h" },
  { label: "Resting HR", value: "52", tone: "text-success", rule: "amber +5 \u00b7 red +10" },
  { label: "Recovery", value: "\u219325", tone: "text-success", rule: "red at 12 or under" },
  { label: "Sitting", value: "3 h", tone: "text-text-primary", rule: "amber 10.6 h \u00b7 red 12 h" },
];

/** Resting rate is read two ways; the tile takes whichever reads worse. */
const againstYourself = [
  ["#34d399", "Within 5 bpm of your 30-day median"],
  ["#FBBF24", "About 5 above, for a few days"],
  ["#f87171", "About 10 above"],
];
const againstLevel = [
  ["50\u201360", "text-success", "Lowest risk sits here"],
  ["75\u201380", "text-warning", "Higher risk even when steady"],
  ["under 50", "text-text-secondary", "Usually adaptation, not a finding"],
];
---

<SubjectLayout subject="sleep-heart">
  <div slot="rail" class="rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-4">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-text-primary">Wearing something else?</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      Oura, Whoop and Garmin reach Verve through Apple Health.
    </p>
    <a href="#connect-wearables" class="mt-2 block text-xs font-medium text-accent-light">How to connect &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <!-- ── The Morning Read — the four Today tiles ─────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="today-tiles" title="The morning read" sub="How the four Today tiles are coloured, and where each line comes from">
    <div class="mt-[18px] rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 lg:grid-cols-4">
        {todayTiles.map((t) => (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/8 bg-white/[0.03] p-3">
            <div class="text-[11px] text-text-secondary">{t.label}</div>
            <div class={`mt-1 text-[19px] font-extrabold ${t.tone}`}>{t.value}</div>
            <div class="mt-[7px] font-mono text-[9.5px] text-text-tertiary">{t.rule}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3.5 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
        Green means inside the range its evidence calls ordinary. A grey dash
        means Verve has not measured it and will not guess. Sleep, resting rate
        and recovery are explained below; the sitting line belongs to Movement.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        Each tile takes its colour from the figure it shows. Green means the
        reading is inside the range its evidence calls ordinary, amber that it
        has crossed one line, red that it has crossed a second. A grey dash
        means Verve has not measured it yet and will not guess. The rules,
        one per tile:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-text-primary">Sleep</strong> &mdash; last night's hours
          asleep against the <strong class="text-text-primary">7 to 9 hour</strong>
          range. Amber under seven; ordinary text above nine, because a long
          night is worth noticing but is not a warning. The drift and
          regularity figures inside the tile's page keep their own scales.
          <a href="#sleep-rhythm">Sleep timing &rarr;</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-text-primary">Resting HR</strong> &mdash; the worse of
          two verdicts. Against your own 30-day median: amber around
          <strong class="text-text-primary">5 bpm above</strong>, red around
          <strong class="text-text-primary">10 above</strong>. Against the level
          itself: amber from <strong class="text-text-primary">75</strong>, red
          from <strong class="text-text-primary">80</strong>. Below your usual, and
          under 50, stay green on purpose &mdash; a rested morning and an
          endurance athlete both land there.
          <a href="#resting-heart-rate">Resting heart rate &rarr;</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-text-primary">Recovery</strong> &mdash; how far your heart
          rate fell in the first minute after the last workout. One cited line:
          red at a fall of <strong class="text-text-primary">12 bpm or less</strong>,
          green above it. There is no amber, because no second line has been
          validated; the tile refuses to invent one.
          <a href="#heart-rate-recovery">Heart rate recovery &rarr;</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-text-primary">Sitting</strong> &mdash; hours today with
          almost no stepping. Amber from <strong class="text-text-primary">10.6
          hours</strong>, red from <strong class="text-text-primary">12</strong>,
          the same two ticks the Movement Balance bar draws.
          <a href="#movement-balance">Movement balance &rarr;</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        Every number above is read from one thresholds file inside the app,
        the same file the tiles read, so this page and the tiles cannot
        disagree about where a line sits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Sleep Rhythm ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="sleep-rhythm" title="Sleep timing" sub="How much your bedtime moves, and the research index behind it">
    <SplitCard eyebrow="In the app" wide>
      <p>
        Three numbers: last night&rsquo;s hours, how far your bedtime drifts
        from one night to the next, and a regularity score from 0 to 100.
        Drift needs five nights. The score needs five comparable days, and a
        day is only comparable once there is a day before it to compare with,
        so five days means six nights.
      </p>
      <div slot="panel">
        <p class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-text-tertiary">Regularity</p>
        <!-- Bands are quintile boundaries of the UK Biobank sample, not guideline lines. -->
        <div class="mt-2.5 flex h-2 overflow-hidden rounded-full">
          <span class="bg-[rgba(244,63,94,0.35)]" style="width: 71.6%"></span>
          <span class="bg-[rgba(251,191,36,0.45)]" style="width: 15.7%"></span>
          <span class="bg-[rgba(52,211,153,0.4)]" style="width: 12.7%"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="relative h-[13px]">
          <span class="absolute top-0.5 h-[9px] w-0.5 bg-text-primary" style="left: 81%"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="relative h-3.5 font-mono text-[9.5px]">
          <span class="absolute left-0 text-text-muted">0</span>
          <span class="absolute -translate-x-1/2 text-warning" style="left: 71.6%">71.6</span>
          <span class="absolute -translate-x-1/2 text-success" style="left: 87.3%">87.3</span>
          <span class="absolute right-0 text-text-muted">100</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-2.5 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
          Where 60,977 UK Biobank adults fell (Windred 2024): the least regular
          fifth below 71.6, the most regular fifth above 87.3, median 81.0. A
          different calculation from Verve&rsquo;s, so this is context, not a
          scale for your score.
        </p>
      </div>
    </SplitCard>

    <div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="Sleep Timing" /></div>
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        Verve leads with the <strong class="text-text-primary">spread of your
        bedtime in minutes</strong>: how far it moves from one night to the
        next. Within-person variability in sleep timing is one of the
        regularity measures the 2025 AHA scientific statement lists, and
        <strong class="text-text-primary">60 minutes</strong> is the cut-point
        its table gives as commonly used &mdash; the text adds that there is no
        agreement on the best threshold. It is in a unit you can act on
        tonight. Clock times are treated as angles on a
        24-hour circle &mdash; a plain average would place 23:50 and 00:10
        nearly a full day apart and report the steadiest sleepers as the most
        erratic.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        Behind it sits the <strong class="text-text-primary">Sleep Regularity
        Index</strong>: the probability of being in the same state, asleep or
        awake, at any two clock times 24 hours apart. It is the stronger
        research metric &mdash; in UK Biobank it out-predicted sleep duration
        for mortality &mdash; but it is a research metric, reported by no
        consumer wearable and named by no guideline for individual care, and
        two accepted calculators disagree on the same nights. So it is the
        number underneath, not the headline. Its median of
        <strong class="text-text-primary">81</strong> is a percentile of one
        cohort, not a clinical threshold.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">The three numbers on the Sleep page.</strong>
        <em>Last night</em> is the summed asleep time of your most recent
        overnight sleep &mdash; awake spells inside the night excluded, naps
        excluded, the same definition Apple Health uses for &ldquo;time
        asleep&rdquo;. <em>Bedtime drift</em> is the night-to-night spread of
        the time you fell asleep, in minutes, over your recent nights; under
        <strong class="text-text-primary">60</strong> is the cut-point the 2025
        AHA statement&rsquo;s table gives as commonly used. The <em>regularity index</em> is the Sleep Regularity
        Index, 0 to 100, with its bands printed on the bar where they fall:
        below <strong class="text-text-primary">71.6</strong> irregular,
        <strong class="text-text-primary">71.6&ndash;87.3</strong> moderately
        irregular, above <strong class="text-text-primary">87.3</strong>
        regular. Those cut-points are the edges of the least and most regular
        fifths of the UK Biobank sample &mdash; the 20th and 80th percentiles
        &mdash; which is why they are printed to a decimal: they are where a
        cohort fell, not lines a guideline drew. The middle half of that
        sample sat between 73.8 and 86.3.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">In Trends, under Over Time.</strong>
        Each lens carries the period&rsquo;s average night by stage &mdash; a
        column per night for the week, per week for the block, per month for
        the year &mdash; and what the body did while asleep: the watch&rsquo;s
        heart rate and breathing rate inside the sleep hours, averaged per
        night and then across nights, against the period before. A night
        belongs to the morning you woke. Stages appear only from a device
        that writes them; a plain asleep record from another writer is shown
        as one block, not divided into stages it never had.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">What it needs, and what it leaves out.</strong>
        The timing measures need a handful of recent recorded nights &mdash;
        the page counts them up for you &mdash; and only recent nights count,
        so a break from wearing the watch means the count starts again.
        Anything that writes sleep to Apple Health works: Apple Watch, Oura,
        Whoop, Garmin. Naps are left out of the timing measures, since a nap
        is not a bedtime. Timing is not hours: a steady short night scores
        better here than an erratic long one, and the two are complementary
        risk markers rather than alternatives. Because accepted SRI
        calculators disagree on the same nights, read your own trend rather
        than comparing the number with anyone else's; and crossing time zones
        registers as irregularity even when your rhythm is steady in local
        time.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div id="connect-wearables" class="mt-4 scroll-mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Connecting Oura, Whoop or Garmin.</strong>
        Verve reads only Apple Health, so a third-party wearable reaches it
        through its own app&rsquo;s Apple Health setting. <em>Oura</em>: the
        Oura app &rarr; menu &rarr; Settings &rarr; Apple Health, and allow it
        to write Sleep, Workouts, Heart Rate and Respiratory Rate.
        <em>Whoop</em>: More &rarr; App Settings &rarr; Integrations &rarr;
        Apple Health, and allow the same categories. <em>Garmin</em>: Garmin
        Connect &rarr; More &rarr; Settings &rarr; Apple Health. Then in Verve,
        Settings &rarr; Apple Health on. How far back an app writes when you
        switch the integration on varies by app; from that day on every night
        arrives. Stages appear when the app writes them; a plain asleep record
        shows as one block. Steps come from whatever writes them to Health:
        the phone in a pocket, an Apple Watch, and Oura or Garmin through
        their apps; Whoop does not write steps. In Verve, Settings &rarr;
        Wearables shows which app wrote the latest sleep, heart-rate and
        workout sample, and when.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {SLEEP_RHYTHM_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Sleep and training ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="sleep-and-training" title="Sleep and training" sub="What the Guide&rsquo;s sleep line is, and what it refuses to claim">
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        The Guide compares two periods of your own record. Since sleep and
        training now come from the same period summary, it can lay them
        side by side: average night asleep, minutes awake in the night,
        sleeping heart rate, bedtime drift and the regularity index, beside
        MVPA, training minutes and strength days for the same two periods.
        Sleep joins the list of movers too &mdash; judged against the adult
        <strong class="text-text-primary">7&ndash;9 hour</strong> band rather
        than a direction, so 6 to 7 hours is an improvement and 8 to 9&frac12;
        is not, while less time awake in the night, a lower sleeping heart
        rate, less bedtime drift and a higher regularity index count the way
        you would expect.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">What the SLEEP line says.</strong>
        One sentence on what moved alongside what &mdash; &ldquo;training rose
        40% and sleep fell 35 minutes, with sleeping heart rate up 3&rdquo;.
        It describes co-movement in your data. It never says one caused the
        other, because two periods of one person cannot show that: a hard
        block can shorten sleep, short sleep can make a block feel harder,
        and a work deadline can do both. The literature below is why the
        pairing is worth reading at all &mdash; short sleep tracks injury and
        worse endurance, and extended sleep has moved performance in small
        trials &mdash; but the line reports, and you interpret.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {SLEEP_TRAINING_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Resting Heart Rate ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="resting-heart-rate" title="Resting heart rate" sub="Measured against your own normal, not a population range">
    <div class="mt-[18px] rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 sm:flex-row sm:gap-[26px]">
        <div class="flex-1">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">Against yourself</p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex flex-col gap-2">
            {againstYourself.map(([dot, text]) => (
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
                <span class="h-[9px] w-[9px] shrink-0 rounded-sm" style={`background: ${dot}`}></span>
                <span class="flex-1 text-[12.5px] text-text-primary">{text}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden w-px bg-white/[0.07] sm:block"></div>
        <div class="flex-1">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">Against the level</p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex flex-col gap-2">
            {againstLevel.map(([range, tone, text]) => (
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
                <span class={`w-[58px] shrink-0 font-mono text-[11px] ${tone}`}>{range}</span>
                <span class="flex-1 text-[12.5px] text-text-primary">{text}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3.5 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
        The tile takes whichever of the two reads worse. Below your usual is
        never coloured, because a rested morning and an endurance athlete both
        land there.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="Resting Heart Rate" /></div>
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        Verve compares today's resting heart rate with the median of your own
        last 30 days. Within <strong class="text-text-primary">5 bpm</strong> is
        ordinary day-to-day variation; around
        <strong class="text-text-primary">5 bpm above</strong>, sustained for a few
        days, is worth noticing; around
        <strong class="text-text-primary">10 above</strong> is a clear departure.
        The instrument shades only the high side, because a low resting rate is
        not a finding &mdash; a rested morning and an adapted endurance athlete
        both land there.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">The level, and what a reading cannot say.</strong>
        Beside the comparison with your own normal, the level itself matters:
        lowest risk sits around <strong class="text-text-primary">50&ndash;60
        bpm</strong>, and above <strong class="text-text-primary">75&ndash;80</strong>
        carries higher risk even when it never moves. Under 50 is common in
        trained people and usually adaptation, worth raising only alongside
        dizziness, fainting or breathlessness. The slow direction counts on its
        own &mdash; a baseline falling over months tracks lower risk, a rising
        one higher, whatever today says. Alcohol, heat, a late meal, poor
        sleep, illness, dehydration, thyroid and some medicines all raise it;
        a wearable reading is not diagnostic.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {RESTING_HR_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Heart Rate Recovery ───────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="heart-rate-recovery" title="Heart rate recovery" sub="One cited line, and why there is no second one">
    <div class="mt-[18px] rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
      <p class="font-mono text-[9px] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-text-tertiary">
        The fall in the first minute, bpm
      </p>
      <!-- Below 12 is a cited finding; above it a gradient, because published
           cut-points span 12 to 30 and Verve will not invent a verdict word. -->
      <div class="mt-3 flex h-[9px] overflow-hidden rounded-full">
        <span class="w-[30%] bg-[rgba(244,63,94,0.45)]"></span>
        <span class="w-[70%] bg-gradient-to-r from-[rgba(251,191,36,0.4)] to-[rgba(52,211,153,0.4)]"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="relative mt-[7px] h-[15px] font-mono text-[10px]">
        <span class="absolute left-0 text-text-muted">0</span>
        <span class="absolute -translate-x-1/2 text-danger" style="left: 30%">12</span>
        <span class="absolute right-0 text-text-muted">40</span>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
        Below the line is a cited finding. Above it is a gradient, not a grade:
        published cut-points span 12 to 30 depending on the study and the
        protocol, so Verve shades and refuses to print a verdict word.
      </p>
    </div>


    <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        Heart rate recovery is the drop from your peak exercising heart rate to
        the rate a fixed time later &mdash; most often one minute. The early part
        of that fall is driven mainly by the vagus nerve switching back on;
        slower sympathetic withdrawal follows over the next few minutes. It is,
        in effect, a read of how quickly your autonomic nervous system lets go
        of effort &mdash; and it predicts mortality independently of how fit you
        are or how much coronary disease you have.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">The one line Verve draws is 12 bpm.</strong>
        In the original study of 2,428 people, a fall of 12 beats or fewer in the
        first minute carried roughly a four-fold higher mortality risk over six
        years. The American Heart Association&rsquo;s exercise-testing statement
        names it the most frequently used cut-point, and a 2025 cohort found the
        same threshold associated with a hazard ratio of about 3.2 for death at
        72 months. That is why the scale on the card is red up to 12 and marks
        nothing else as a verdict.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Why there is no &ldquo;good&rdquo; number.</strong>
        You will see 18&ndash;20 bpm quoted as a healthy recovery. It is a lay
        benchmark, not a validated norm: published cut-points for one-minute
        recovery run anywhere from 12 to 30 bpm depending on the study, the
        population and the protocol. What the evidence does show is that the
        relationship is not a switch. Risk falls steeply as recovery improves to
        somewhere around 15&ndash;20 bpm, then the curve flattens &mdash; beyond
        that range, extra recovery buys little further protection. Verve shades
        the scale above 12 from amber towards green to say exactly that, and
        deliberately draws no second line.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">The number depends on what you do after you stop.</strong>
        Recovery measured while walking slowly is different from recovery
        measured sitting or lying down; passive recovery produces larger drops
        and needs higher cut-points. The 12 bpm line was derived with an active
        walking cool-down. Apple Watch measures recovery in whatever you happen
        to do after ending a workout, which is one more reason the comparison
        Verve leads with is <strong class="text-text-primary">you against your own
        30-day median</strong> &mdash; the same rule as resting heart rate &mdash;
        rather than you against a table. Your protocol is at least consistent
        with itself. Recovery also declines with age and is higher in
        adolescents, so a fixed adult line is not applied to younger users.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Shown but not scored.</strong>
        The line was measured after hard effort, and how far the rate falls
        scales with how high it went. A walk that ended below your own Zone 2
        floor still has a fall, and the page shows it, in grey, with
        &ldquo;ended below Zone 2&rdquo; beside it &mdash; but it is not judged
        against the 12 bpm line, not counted among the readings above or below
        it, and not in the week average. It is what the watch measured; it is
        not a fitness reading.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Where the number comes from.</strong>
        Two places, and the card says which. Apple Watch stores a one-minute
        recovery for a workout when it sees an undisturbed minute after you tap
        End; Verve reads that value as it is. But the watch keeps recording your
        heart rate for about three minutes after every workout whether or not it
        stores a recovery &mdash; a run followed straight by a walk gets none
        &mdash; and the Fitness app draws that as &ldquo;Post-Workout Heart
        Rate&rdquo;. For a recent workout with no stored reading, Verve takes the
        same two points the Fitness app shows, the rate at the end of the workout
        and the rate one minute later, and reports the difference, marked
        <em>from workout HR</em>. Nothing is smoothed, fitted or assumed: two
        readings, one subtraction.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Why Verve&rsquo;s number can differ from the one on the Fitness chart.</strong>
        Open a workout in Fitness and you will see a number under each minute of
        the post-workout chart. Those numbers summarise the chart. They are not
        the recovery measurement, and subtracting one from the next gives a
        figure nobody validated. Apple keeps the recovery separately, worked out
        its own way: the highest rate it saw once recovery had begun, minus the
        rate one minute later. The two disagree because they do not start at the
        same moment. The chart starts the instant you tap End; the stored figure
        starts a little later, once the watch is satisfied that recovery has
        actually started, so it begins from a lower rate. On one run here the
        chart read 150 then 137, a fall of 13, while the watch stored a fall of
        25 for the same minute.
      </p>
      <figure class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30">
          <img
            src="/science/apple-post-workout-hr.png"
            alt="The Post-Workout Heart Rate chart in Apple Fitness. Below the chart, three labels: 150 BPM at 07:00, 137 BPM at 1 MIN, 126 BPM at 2 MIN. The chart's range is 152 at the top and 123 at the bottom."
            width="760"
            height="275"
            class="block w-full"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
          <strong class="font-semibold text-text-secondary">Apple Fitness, the same run.</strong>
          The three labels summarise the three minutes of the chart above them.
          Reading the first two as a recovery gives 13 bpm. That subtraction is
          the reader&rsquo;s, not Apple&rsquo;s: nothing on this screen claims it.
        </figcaption>

        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30">
          <img
            src="/science/verve-hr-recovery.png"
            alt="The same run in Verve, on the heart rate recovery page. Sunday 13 September, Running, 07:00, 139 falling to 114, a drop of 25 bpm shown in green."
            width="760"
            height="163"
            class="block w-full"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
          <strong class="font-semibold text-text-secondary">Verve, the same run.</strong>
          25 bpm, which is the figure the watch stored for that workout, shown
          unchanged. The two rates beside it are the ones Apple measured it
          between: the highest rate once recovery had begun, and the rate a
          minute later.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Which one Verve shows, and why that is the right one.</strong>
        The stored figure, unchanged and unrounded, for two reasons. It is the
        number Apple itself commits to for that workout, so Verve is not
        second-guessing your watch with arithmetic of its own. And it is measured
        the way the research measured it &mdash; from the effort down through the
        first minute &mdash; which is what makes the 12&nbsp;bpm line above mean
        anything at all. A number read off a chart cannot be compared with a
        study that measured something else. When the watch stores nothing, Verve
        says so by marking the reading as its own, so you always know which of
        the two you are looking at.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Where the readings live.</strong>
        The Activity page shows this week, Monday to today, every reading
        under the day it happened. Earlier weeks are in Trends, under Over Time, as the
        period average beside resting HR, the way every other measure works.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Your own usual.</strong>
        The app does not print a &ldquo;vs usual&rdquo; figure; the reading
        is the reading. The yardstick worth keeping in mind is the median of
        your own recoveries over the last 30 days &mdash; the rule the resting
        heart rate tile uses &mdash; because there is no population
        &ldquo;good&rdquo; to compare with: above 12 bpm the evidence is a
        gradient, not a second line. A recovery is one number per workout,
        not per day, so it takes a handful of workouts before that median
        means anything, and a single low reading after a hard, hot or
        back-to-back session is a session, not a trend.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {HEART_RATE_RECOVERY_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>
</SubjectLayout>
